Expected elements: present

Everything required is on the surface: the near-black identity bar with the `aztec` exit, truncated hash, `Partial`, `block 101`, both stepping directions, scrubber, `128 / 1315` and the phase rail; `CODE` positioned at line 32; `CALL TRACE | EVENT LOG` as one tab strip with Call Trace open; `VALUES` as a pane below it; and `TRANSACTION`, whose first labelled row is `DATA` carrying a filled `Synthetic demo data` badge over the producer's own sentences. No band above the bar, no light chrome, no spinner.

This theme is designed, not inverted — there are real surface levels, a cyan accent family, an amber/green status family and a genuine editor palette. On the fair question the brief raises: chromatically the provenance row is not too quiet. Its badge is the only inverted, near-black-on-light element in the pane, making it the highest-contrast object there.

What is below bar is role discipline. Amber means both "the trace is Partial" and "this value changed at this step". Cyan means link, active tab, active phase and scrubber progress, so `FETCHING` reads as clickable. Badge fill encodes nothing — `Trace ready` is filled green and its peer `Not observable`, one line above it in the same EXECUTIONS row, is outline grey. And the new branch marks get no colour at all: `⊘` and `⊙` sit at the same neutral grey as the inert `·` markers beside them.

Fixes: separate the changed-value hue from the completeness hue; give ran/did-not-run a tonal difference.

Rating: 6/10

